    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2025/04/nike-vomero-18-review-2025.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The 44/34 mm maximal stack (ZoomX over ReactX) delivers balanced impact protection with a cushioned feel, but it runs firmer than expected rather than soft/mushy; heel transitions can feel a bit clunky while forefoot roll is smooth.",
        "Responsiveness: Despite the high stack, the foam feels moderately responsive with a mildly snappy forefoot rocker and some rebound, making the shoe feel lighter than its listed weight, but not fast-day lively.",
        "Stability: It is a neutral shoe with mild guidance from a wide platform, limited midfoot narrowing, and heel/forefoot flare; heel hold is helped by a moderately stiff heel counter and secure upper, though the lateral heel flare can bias landings medially.",
        "Durability: Outsole durability is the weak point (early lug wear and even an outsole piece coming off by about 45 miles), while the midsole has held up well and maintained its ride feel."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2025/02/nike-vomero-18-multi-tester-review-with.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The Vomero 18 is positioned as max-cushion (46 mm heel / 36 mm forefoot, ZoomX over ReactX) with a soft, plush ride and strong impact protection; testers describe it as very cushy and comfortable without feeling mushy.",
        "Responsiveness: The midsole is called reactive, but overall feel trends toward compliance over snap, with comments about a somewhat blocky transition and requests for more all-ZoomX bounce/energy return.",
        "Stability: Testers report a deep, stable platform with secure midfoot and heel hold; the heel counter is solid but not fully rigid, giving reliable daily stability while prioritizing comfort over race-like lateral control.",
        "Durability: The outsole has more segmented, strategic rubber coverage and the upper is robust/padded, but the provided review text does not include long-term outsole wear or material longevity outcomes."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/nike-vomero-18-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The shoe is built for max comfort with a very high 46 mm heel / 36 mm forefoot stack and a dual-foam midsole (ZoomX over ReactX) that emphasizes soft impact protection.",
        "Responsiveness: ZoomX is described as bouncy and Nike claims ReactX improves energy return by 13%, but the overall ride is still more comfort-focused than sharp/snappy due to the heavier build.",
        "Stability: The provided text gives limited direct stability testing detail; it notes rocker geometry and added heel/collar padding but does not provide a clear torsional-rigidity or heel-counter assessment.",
        "Durability: Durability is presented as a strength via a high-abrasion rubber outsole, waffle lug pattern, and added flex grooves aimed at longer wear and reliable traction."
      ]
    }
